  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V19: AC induction motors. Why must an induction-motor rotor lag the rotating field? (design)\r",
    "causalExplanation": "First, how can a field turn when nothing moves? The fixed outer part of the motor is called the stator. It has coils spaced round the rotor. Each coil carries an alternating current, but the currents are out of step, so they peak one after another. One coil alone would just make a field that grows, shrinks and reverses in place. But with the coils peaking in turn, the strongest field moves round the ring. It's like lights on a sign that chase round a circle. Each bulb only blinks on and off, but the bright spot travels. So the field's direction turns steadily, at a speed set by the supply frequency. The turning part, the rotor, is often a squirrel cage. It's a ring of metal bars, joined at both ends by metal rings. No wires, no brushes, no connection to the supply. But every pair of bars, with the end rings, makes a closed loop. And a closed loop is exactly what induction needs. Now let the field turn while the rotor is still at rest. The field sweeps past the bars, so the flux through each loop of the cage keeps changing. By Faraday's law, that induces an emf in each loop. The bars are thick metal with very little resistance, so large currents flow. These are induced currents. Nothing supplies them directly. They're made by the field moving past the rotor.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Here's that arrangement. The field turns, and the rotor follows it round, even though nothing is connected to the rotor. Many desk fans run on a motor like this. Now watch the speeds. On a fifty hertz supply, this motor's field turns at fifteen hundred revolutions per minute. The rotor turns at about fourteen twenty five. It never catches up. Is that just friction slowing it, or does the rotor need to lag to be pushed at all? Make your prediction. It needs the lag. The rotor only gets a push while it lags, and it always needs some push, to overcome friction and turn its load. First, how can a field turn when nothing moves? The fixed outer part of the motor is called the stator. It has coils spaced round the rotor. Each coil carries an alternating current, but the currents are out of step, so they peak one after another. One coil alone would just make a field that grows, shrinks and reverses in place. But with the coils peaking in turn, the strongest field moves round the ring. It's like lights on a sign that chase round a circle. Each bulb only blinks on and off, but the bright spot travels. So the field's direction turns steadily, at a speed set by the supply frequency. The turning part, the rotor, is often a squirrel cage. It's a ring of metal bars, joined at both ends by metal rings. No wires, no brushes, no connection to the supply. But every pair of bars, with the end rings, makes a closed loop. And a closed loop is exactly what induction needs.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v18's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"; the painted cage plate in side view beside the coded ring); the chip becomes the heading: \"Why must an induction-motor rotor lag the rotating field?\". On \"Here's that arrangement\" the six-coil sketch redraws as a four-pole ring (twelve coils; slate pole markers N, S, N, S alternating at 90°, turning together, the dashed arrow from one N), with a grey tag (30 px) \"4 poles: field turns at 1500 rev/min (50 Hz)\" and a small grey bridge tag (30 px) \"video 18's sketch: 2 poles, 3000 rev/min\". On \"nothing is connected\" the navy bracket \"no wires to the rotor\" pulses. On \"desk fans\" the desk fan plate (`phys-y11-desk-fan-v1.png`, about 360 px tall) slides in at the right with its blades layer (`phys-y11-desk-fan-blades-v1.png`) turning; tag \"many desk fans: small induction motors\"; it slides out. On \"fifteen hundred\" a grey readout (52 px) under the field arrow \"field: 1500 rev/min\". On \"fourteen twenty five\" a second readout under the rotor's mark \"rotor: ≈ 1425 rev/min\". On \"up\" the two markers circle together with the rotor's mark slipping steadily behind (a growing angle between them, wrapping round); amber hand tag \"always behind\". On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside two chips: \"friction just slows it\" and \"it must lag to be pushed at all\". On \"lag\" the second chip ticks. On \"always needs some push\" the first chip is crossed in red with a red hand tag \"friction needs a push; the lag makes the push\".",
    "understandingCheck": "Here's that arrangement. The field turns, and the rotor follows it round, even though nothing is connected to the rotor. Many desk fans run on a motor like this. Now watch the speeds. On a fifty hertz supply, this motor's field turns at fifteen hundred revolutions per minute. The rotor turns at about fourteen twenty five. It never catches up. Is that just friction slowing it, or does the rotor need to lag to be pushed at all? Make your prediction. It needs the lag. The rotor only gets a push while it lags, and it always needs some push, to overcome friction and turn its load. Your turn. Explain why the rotor of an AC induction motor that's driving a load can never rotate at the same speed as the stator's rotating magnetic field. Pause here to answer. Last one. An induction motor runs at a steady speed. The load on it is increased. Explain what happens to the rotor's speed, the slip, and the current induced in the rotor. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V19: AC induction motors. Why must an induction-motor rotor lag the rotating field? (design)\r\n\r\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 190 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\r\n\r\nNarration 6,462 characters, 16 scenes, about 558 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v18's final frame.\r\n\r\nScope: nineteenth video of Year 12 Physics Module 6 (Electromagnetism, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"How has knowledge about the Motor Effect been applied to technological advances?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m6-v07.md` and `phys-y12-m6-v11.md`. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lesson read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extract `work/brief168/module6-lesson16.sa.txt`, hash checked against the live file): orient and retrieve (disconnected-rotor prediction, warm-up, intentions, vocabulary, cross-lesson links), explain the rotating field (aluminium cylinder in a ring of electromagnets, stator, squirrel-cage rotor, four steps, HSC tip \"three stages to mention\", why slip is essential, n_s = 120f/p and percentage slip, true/false), power-station generators (used by v18), the interactive, calculate slip and speed (marked \"Beyond the syllabus\"; worked example 4 poles, 50 Hz, 1425 RPM, 5.0 per cent; 2-pole 3000 RPM; poles versus pole pairs), apply and consolidate (Activity 1, odd one out, Activity 2, \"Analyse 6 marks\" power station, \"Compare 5 marks\" DC versus induction torque), practice (MC, Q1 4-pole 1440 RPM, Q2 load increase, model answers), review (Tesla's 1888 motor): `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module6/lesson16.sa.html`, SHA256 `352d49dc5beecf7f5feb79636134a4e6f4a0b4a00e3bda80b9139052d8959eed` (recomputed 6 Nov; matches the route). Also used: lesson 10's `induction-motor-overview` (one coil alone gives a pulsating field; \"Analyse 6 marks\" on the induction motor's limiting case), read in full for v16 and v18 (`module6/lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `736384e8c6b6ea3e705767d723a0ee76e77ccceae7ab2d40f7f297065de8d36f`). Route entry: `phys-y12-m6-v19`, sections `explain-rotating-field`, `calculate-slip-and-speed`. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Explain rotating stator field, induced rotor current, torque and slip causally; contrast synchronous speed and rotor speed. Stop before full three-phase winding design.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m6-v20: How can a moving conductor be braked without contact?\"\r\n\r\nOwner rules applied: as v07 and v11 (2017 syllabus quoted exactly; one main learner question; no people, hands, faces or body outlines, and no people in analogies (the rotating field is compared to chasing lights and slip to a sailing boat, both drawn as coded diagrams with no figures); the squirrel-cage rotor and desk fan are painted plates (one new, one reused); the stator ring, rotating field, rotor loops, currents, forces and the speed readouts are coded; every number recalculated in Python; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed mistake; original attempts; optional key notes; Australian spelling; no em dashes). Recalled, not re-taught: v07 (motor effect: a current in a field feels a force), v10 (Faraday's law), v11 (Lenz's law: the induced effect opposes the change; \"going away, it pulls back\"), v16 and v17 (the DC motor, brushes and split ring), v18 (AC; a generator under load).\r\n\r\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 6 Electromagnetism; ledger id `P6-IQ4-02`), quoted from the route JSON `syllabusActions`:\r\n\r\n- `P6-IQ4-02`: \"analyse the operation of simple DC and AC generators and AC induction motors (ACSPH110)\"\r\n\r\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 57)\r\n\r\n**Boundary:** starts on v18's final frame (a stator ring with a rotating field arrow, a coded end-on cage rotor inside with its mark turning slightly behind, the cage plate in side view beside it, \"?\"). Teaches: the observation (Revision 2, root review: the ring is drawn with four poles, field 1500 revolutions per minute (site), rotor about 1425 while it drives its load and friction); the stator and the rotating field (several coils, AC out of step, the strongest point moving round like chasing lights; one coil alone only pulses; speed set by the supply frequency); the squirrel-cage rotor (bars and end rings, closed loops, no connection); induced rotor currents (field sweeping past the loops, Faraday); torque (motor-effect forces; Lenz: oppose the relative motion, so the rotor is dragged along with the field); why the rotor must lag (at the field's speed: no relative motion, no flux change, no current, no torque; slip defined, necessary); load (more slip, more current, more torque, a new lower speed); typical numbers labelled beyond the syllabus (the same 4-pole motor at 50 Hz: 1500; rotor 1425; slip 75, 5.0 per cent); the sailing-boat picture of slip; comparison with the DC motor (supplied versus induced current, split ring versus rotating field, no brushes, low maintenance, slip). Stops before three-phase winding design, phase diagrams, torque-slip curves, synchronous motors and generator load angle. Magnetic braking is v20's.\r\n\r\n## Decisions\r\n\r\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"Why must an induction-motor rotor lag the rotating field?\" is causal. The answer, built in order: the rotor's current is induced only while the field slides past it (4); that current's force drags the rotor along (5); at the field's speed nothing slides past, so there is no current and no force (6).\r\n- **Hook (v18's handoff and the site's prediction box; Revision 2).** The speed readouts (the site's 1500 and about 1425 revolutions per minute, on a ring drawn with four poles so the speed matches the picture) make the lag a measured fact. The prediction contrasts \"friction just slows it\" with \"it must lag to be pushed at all\"; the answer is that the rotor gets a push only while it lags, and a working motor always needs some push, against friction and its load. Revision 1's claim that it could not reach the field's speed even with no friction and no load is withdrawn (brief 190): in that ideal limit the rotor would approach the field's speed, needing no torque (author note e). The desk-fan plate grounds the device in one sentence (many desk fans use small induction motors).\r\n- **Rotating field, qualitatively (route stop \"before full three-phase winding design\"; lesson 10 \"Alternation in one coil alone gives a pulsating field\").** Twelve coils round the ring in three phase groups (a four-pole winding, drawn schematically), each group with AC, peaking one after another; the four poles (N, S, N, S) move round together; one coil alone only grows, shrinks and reverses in place. Analogy: chasing lights on a sign (each bulb blinks in place, the bright spot travels). No phase diagram or 120° arithmetic is shown.\r\n- **Torque direction from Lenz (site HSC tip and four steps; lesson 10 \"Analyse 6 marks\" step 4).** The narration names the change as \"the field sliding past the rotor\" and says the forces act to reduce that sliding, so they drag the rotor along. This links to v11's \"Going away, it pulls back\" (the field's poles move away from the bars, so the bars are pulled after them).\r\n- **Slip necessary, and load (site \"Why slip is essential\", Q2; site note 1).** The limiting-case argument is narrated as a thought experiment (6). The load scene states that the rotor settles at a slightly lower speed with more slip and does not return to its old speed (site Q2's model answer says \"restoring the rotor speed\"; site note 1).\r\n- **Pole count matches the picture (Revision 2, brief 190 should fix; root review 7 Nov).** The site's numbers are kept (site is the source of truth): field 1500 revolutions per minute, rotor about 1425, 5.0 per cent slip. The drawing changes instead: from scene 1 the stator ring is a four-pole ring (twelve coils in three phase groups; slate pole markers N, S, N, S alternating at 90° that turn together), with the grey tag \"4 poles: field turns at 1500 rev/min (50 Hz)\". Scene 8 works the same four-pole motor. v18's final frame showed a simple six-coil sketch and v18 scene 9 quotes a 2-pole 3000 rev/min machine, so scene 1 redraws the ring as it opens and adds a small grey bridge tag \"video 18's sketch: 2 poles, 3000 rev/min\" (screen only, no narration).\r\n- **Numbers labelled beyond the syllabus (site section 5 banner: \"Synchronous speed n_s = 120f/p, percentage-slip calculations and three-phase detail are extension\").** The route lists `calculate-slip-and-speed`, so scene 8 shows the site's worked example (4 poles, 50 Hz: 1500; rotor 1425: slip 75 and 5.0 per cent) with a grey \"beyond the syllabus\" tag; no formula is relied on in the attempts (open decision 1). The word \"synchronous speed\" is shown on screen as the name of the field's speed (route \"contrast synchronous speed and rotor speed\") and said once.\r\n- **Analogy (scene 9, new): a sailing boat going straight downwind.** See \"Memory aid check\". People are not drawn: the boat is a coded silhouette with a square sail and wind arrows.\r\n- **Comparison with the DC motor (site \"Compare 5 marks\", lesson 10 compare section).** Shared: motor-effect forces on currents in a field. Different: supplied current with brushes and a split ring versus induced current with a rotating field; no brushes or sparking, low maintenance; slip exists only in the induction motor.\r\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (Revision 2: \"that is driving a load\"): explain why the rotor cannot rotate at the field's speed. A2: a load increase: the rotor's speed, the slip and the induced current. Crossed: A1 \"friction causes the lag\" (friction is why a push is needed; the lag is what makes the push); A2 \"the rotor speeds back up to its old speed\".\r\n- **Handoff (route next).** Scene 15 flips the arrangement: hold the magnet still and move the metal. A copper pendulum plate (requested in `phys-y12-m6-v20.md`) swings into a field region; the same induced currents now drag it back. See \"Final frame\".\r\n- **Colour key (root decision 1):** stator field slate (dashed resultant arrow, slate pole tint on the coils); induced rotor emf and current rose; supply current in the stator coils orange; forces and torque amber; speed readouts grey.\r\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above; speed readouts grey at full ink; slip and the grip bar amber; load arrow amber outline; framed results in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\r\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current working line at least 44 px (scene 8 and the feedback rows 52 px; framed results 60 px); live readouts (field and rotor speeds, slip, the grip bar label) about 52 px; chain chips, comparison rows and axis labels 32 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\r\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** Scene 1 is v18's final frame, with the delivered squirrel-cage rotor plate (side view) beside the coded ring; the desk fan plate grounds the device in scene 1; the cage plate fills scene 3; the delivered copper pendulum plate carries the handoff.\r\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is scene 6's rotor-speed slider: the rotor is pushed from 1350 towards 1500 rev/min while live readouts update (slip 10, 5.0, 2.0, 0 per cent) and an amber \"grip\" (torque) bar shrinks to nothing (illustrative small-slip model, author note g); the sliding field lines slow and freeze. Scenes 1, 4, 5 and 7 keep the live speed readouts and the sliding-field overlay.\r\n- **Stage changes (Revision 2).** Revision 1 kept the ring and rotor for scenes 4 to 7 (about 139 s). Now: scene 4 the ring with the rotor at rest; scene 5 a camera push to two bars of the rotor (forces and currents large, v11 inset); scene 6 a clear change to the what-if panel with the speed slider; scene 7 the ring with large speed dials and the load arrow. No set-up runs past about 60 s.\r\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a small diagram in one move. On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the conditions, the diagram shows the answer, the key result is framed and one classic slip is crossed.\r\n- **Site sources used:** prediction box (1); aluminium-cylinder demonstration (1, as the fact that nothing is connected); stator and rotor definitions (2, 3); four steps (4, 5); HSC tip (2, 4, 5); \"Why slip is essential\" (6); worked example (8); Activity 1 (6, 10); odd one out (3: no slip rings to the rotor); \"Compare 5 marks\" (10); Q1 (A1 style); Q2 (7, A2; site note 1); review (6). Lesson 10: pulsating single coil (2), limiting case (1, 6), maintenance comparison (10). Not used: the power-station analysis (v18), the interactive, the Q1 calculation (1440 RPM, 4.0 per cent; checked), poles versus pole pairs detail, synchronous generator load angle.\r\n- **Site notes (quoted from site lesson 16, read 6 Nov; for Codex to log in `site-corrections-phys-y12.md`):**\r\n  1. Section `practice-induction-motors`, Q2 model answer: \"The larger rotor current interacts with the stator field to produce a greater magnetic torque, restoring the rotor speed and matching the increased load demand (1 mark).\" The rotor does not return to its former speed: it settles at a slightly lower speed, with more slip, where the larger torque matches the larger load (the larger slip is what sustains the larger current). Proposed fix: \"...to produce a greater magnetic torque, which matches the increased load at a slightly lower rotor speed (larger slip) (1 mark).\"\r\n  2. Section `review-and-revisit`: \"At the start you were asked about Tesla's 1888 induction motor\". The opening prediction box asks about a squirrel-cage rotor with no connection, not about Tesla's 1888 motor. Proposed fix: \"At the start you were asked how a rotor with no electrical connection can turn\" (or add the Tesla framing to the opening).\r\n  Checked and correct: worked example (1500 RPM; 75 RPM; 5.0 per cent; 3000 RPM); Q1 (1500 RPM; 60 RPM; 4.0 per cent); MC 3000 RPM; odd one out (slip rings to the rotor is false); lesson 10's comparison and limiting case.\r\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) the rotor follows the turning field but, while it drives a load against friction, never reaches its speed (1); (2) coils round the stator carry AC peaking one after another, so the field's strongest direction turns at a speed set by the supply frequency (2); (3) the cage's bars and end rings form closed loops with no connection to the supply (3); (4) the field sweeping past changes the flux through those loops, inducing emfs and large currents (4); (5) those currents in the field feel forces; by Lenz's law they act to reduce the sliding, so they drag the rotor along with the field (5); (6) at the field's speed there would be no sliding, no flux change, no current and no torque, so friction and the load slow the rotor until it lags: slip is necessary to produce torque (6); (7) more load means more slip, faster flux change, more current and more torque, at a lower speed (7); (8) slip is typically a few per cent (8); (9) unlike the DC motor, the rotor's current is induced and the field turns, so no brushes or split ring, at the price of slip (10).\r\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v18's AC and generator; v07's motor effect; v10's Faraday's law; v11's Lenz's law; v16 and v17's DC motor (brushes, split ring); Year 11 frequency.\r\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** stator (the fixed outer part, with coils, 2); rotating field (a magnetic field whose direction turns steadily, 2); rotor (the turning part, 3); squirrel-cage rotor (metal bars joined at both ends by metal rings, 3); induced current (a current made by a changing flux, with no supply connected, 4); slip (how far the rotor's speed falls behind the field's speed, 6); synchronous speed (the field's speed, on screen in scene 8 and said once).\r\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. The kit family `m6-induction-slip` (schematic resultant field, illustrative 20 per cent slip, no winding phase detail) is the candidate support for scenes 1, 4 to 7; set its slip to the scene's values (5 per cent in scenes 1 and 8; larger, schematic, in scene 7) and keep its \"no winding phase detail\" limit.\r\n- **New explainer motion:** **stator ring** (Revision 2: a four-pole ring of twelve coded coils in three phase groups, each group's fill pulsing with its own sine, peaking in turn; four slate pole markers N, S, N, S at 90° turning together, with a dashed arrow from one N; a \"chasing lights\" mode with a ring of dots lighting in sequence); **cage rotor** (the plate in side view for scene 3; a coded end-on rotor for scenes 4 to 7 with bars as dots round its rim, rose dots and crosses for bar currents, an index mark that turns); **sliding-field overlay** (slate field lines drawn across the rotor that move relative to it at the slip rate; when the speeds match they freeze relative to the rotor); **speed readouts** (two grey dials or counters \"field\" and \"rotor\" in revolutions per minute); **force arrows** (amber tangential arrows on the current-carrying bars); **what-if** (the rotor sped up to the field's speed: the sliding lines freeze, bar currents fade, arrows vanish, the rotor decelerates); **load arrow** (amber outline counter-torque on the shaft); **sailing panel** (coded boat silhouette with a square sail, wind arrows of fixed length, a relative-wind arrow at the sail that shrinks to zero as the boat's speed approaches the wind's); `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 11 and 13); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\r\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the stator ring with the rotor centre-left; speed readouts and chain chips on the right; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\r\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\r\n  (a) Rotating field: three-phase AC in three coil pairs spaced 120° round the stator gives a resultant field of constant magnitude turning at the synchronous speed; drawn qualitatively, no phase arithmetic (route stop). Single-phase fans use a split phase or shaded poles to start; not discussed beyond \"many desk fans use small induction motors\".\r\n  (b) Synchronous speed n_s = 120f/p (p = number of poles): 4 poles, 50 Hz → 1500 revolutions per minute (25 per second), the drawn ring throughout (Revision 2, root review); 2 poles would give 3000 (v18's power-station point, bridged by a grey tag in scene 1). Rotor 1425: slip 75, 75/1500 = 5.0 per cent. Scene 8 is shown with a \"beyond the syllabus\" tag.\r\n  (c) Torque direction: relative to the rotor the field moves forward; induced currents oppose the relative motion (Lenz), so the force on the rotor is in the field's direction of rotation. On the end-on rotor, bars just \"behind\" the leading pole carry currents shown as rose dots or crosses (out of or into the screen); amber arrows tangential, forward.\r\n  (d) Load response: increased load torque → rotor decelerates → slip increases → rate of flux change in the rotor increases → rotor emf and current increase → electromagnetic torque increases → new equilibrium at higher slip (lower speed), in the normal operating range (small slip). Torque-slip curve beyond pull-out not discussed.\r\n  (e) Revision 2 (brief 190 should fix): zero slip means zero sustained induction torque. It does not by itself imply slowing if both friction and load are exactly zero; in that ideal limit the rotor approaches the field's speed from below. Any real motor has friction and usually a load, so it needs torque, and torque needs slip. Narration now says \"the rotor only gets a push while it lags, and it always needs some push, to overcome friction and turn its load\" (1), \"So why can't a working motor catch up?\" (6), and A1 asks about a motor \"that is driving a load\". The mnemonic \"No slip, no grip\" (no slip, no torque) is kept.\r\n  (f) Pronunciations: \"stator\" (STAY-tor); \"rotor\"; \"synchronous\" (SIN-kruh-nus); \"emf\" letter by letter; \"fifteen hundred\", \"fourteen twenty five\" for 1425; \"fourteen twenty five\" kept for 1425 (Revision 2, root review).\r\n  (g) Speed slider (Revision 2, scene 6): rotor 1350, 1425, 1470, 1500 rev/min against the 1500 rev/min field: slip 10, 5.0, 2.0, 0 per cent; the \"grip\" (torque) bar uses a small-slip model in which torque is proportional to slip (1.00, 0.50, 0.20, 0 of the 10 per cent value), tagged \"illustrative\".\r\n- **Open decisions for root:**\r\n  - **1. Scene 8 (beyond the syllabus).** The route lists the site's slip calculation; the site marks it as extension. Keep as a short tagged scene (current design), or cut it and keep \"a few percent\" in scene 6 (saves about 380 characters).\r\n  - **2. Plates.** Closed in Revision 2: `y12p-squirrel-cage-rotor-v1.png` and `y12p-copper-pendulum-v1.png` were delivered in brief 190 and inspected. The cage plate is side view only; end-on views are coded.\r\n  - **3. Optional beats** the learning review may cut: scene 9's analogy (if it slows the video), the desk-fan sentence in scene 1, scene 10's maintenance sentence.\r\nMain question: *A t